WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.750
Wind and current decide which docking plan you use.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.825
If the wind is blowing onto the dock, it’s the easy one.

3
00:00:08.075 --> 00:00:13.150
Stop parallel, a few feet off, and let the wind set you gently alongside.

4
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:21.625
If the wind is blowing off the dock, come in at a steeper angle, get a bow line or a spring on quickly, and use it to work her in.

5
00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:24.850
Current from ahead is the easiest of all.

6
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:30.175
Current from astern is the hard one, because it’s pushing you faster than you think.

7
00:00:30.425 --> 00:00:32.425
What should you do if you can?

8
00:00:33.425 --> 00:00:36.075
Turn around, and come in against it.

9
00:00:36.325 --> 00:00:37.900
And leaving.

10
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:43.025
Motor gently ahead against a bow spring with the wheel turned toward the dock.

11
00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:46.525
The stern swings out, and then you back away clear.

12
00:00:46.775 --> 00:00:48.700
That’s lesson seven.

13
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.700
Prop walk: a right-hand prop walks to port in reverse.

14
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.900
The stern swings wide.

15
00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.525
Slow, into the wind or current, lines ready.

16
00:00:58.775 --> 00:01:01.450
Next time: the lines themselves.
